WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.100
A database secret has a lifecycle: creation, activation, use, expiration, rotation, revocation, retirement, and sometimes emergency replacement.

2
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.520
Rotation should reduce risk without causing avoidable outages.

3
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:25.260
That means teams need to know which applications, jobs, migrations, notebooks, dashboards, and pipelines consume the credential.

4
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.400
A secret with unknown consumers is difficult to rotate safely because nobody knows what will break.

5
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.800
Staged or dual-credential rotation patterns can reduce downtime risk.

6
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:44.860
The database may accept an old and new credential during a controlled transition, or the application may be updated to read a new secret before the old one is revoked.

7
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.280
Connection pool draining, application restarts, worker reloads, and scheduled jobs must be considered.

8
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:59.140
Rotation is not finished until old credentials are no longer accepted and consumers are verified on the new path.

9
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.380
Migration coordination and rollback planning matter.

10
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:09.920
A deployment may include application code, database schema changes, secret updates, and infrastructure changes.

11
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:17.780
If rotation fails halfway through, teams need a safe plan that preserves availability without leaving both credentials active indefinitely.

12
00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:28.840
Emergency replacement after exposure may move faster, but it still needs owner communication, scope assessment, revocation, and verification that affected systems are using the replacement.

13
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.040
Documenting credential owners and consumers is practical security work.

14
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:39.380
The owner knows why the secret exists, who can approve changes, when it should rotate, and what systems rely on it.

15
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.760
Consumers are the services, jobs, or humans allowed to retrieve it.

16
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.340
Without that map, rotation becomes a high-risk manual exercise.

17
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:56.240
With it, rotation becomes an expected maintenance workflow that improves security and reliability together.
